      "nets": [
        "GND"
      ],
      "observed": "The isolated CAN transceivers U10 and U11 have both their GND1 and GND2 pins connected to the same common GND net.",
      "expected": "For the specified isolated CAN buses, the transceiver logic-side ground (GND1) and bus-side ground (GND2) should be separate isolated ground nets; tying them together defeats the galvanic isolation provided by the isolated CAN transceiver.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"80\") (name \"GND\") ... (node (ref \"U10\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"GND1\") (pintype \"power_in\")) (node (ref \"U10\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") (pintype \"power_in\")) (node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"GND1\") (pintype \"power_in\")) (node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") (pintype \"power_in\")) ...)"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (`CAN0`, `CAN1`), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (`U8`, `U9`) through an isolated CAN transceiver (`U11`/companion)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "CAN0 and CAN1 are not galvanically isolated as intended. Ground loops, motor-side noise, and common-mode faults can propagate directly between the CAN bus side and the CM4 logic side, contrary to the isolated CAN architecture.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": [
        "SW6",
        "R34",
        "R35"
      ],
      "pins": [
        "SW6.1",
        "SW6.2",
        "SW6.3",
        "SW6.4",
        "R34.2",
        "R35.2"
      ],
      "nets": [
        "/CAN0_N",
        "/CAN0_P",
        "/CAN1_N",
        "/CAN1_P",
        "Net-(R34-Pad2)",
        "Net-(R35-Pad2)"
      ],
      "observed": "The 120 Ω resistors R34 and R35 connect /CAN0_N and /CAN1_N to SW6 pins 1 and 2, while SW6 pins 3 and 4 connect to /CAN1_P and /CAN0_P respectively. With the common-row DIP-switch pin numbering implied by SW7, closing SW6 connects /CAN0_N to /CAN1_P and /CAN1_N to /CAN0_P.",
      "expected": "A switchable CAN termination should place each 120 Ω resistor across its own bus: /CAN0_N to /CAN0_P and /CAN1_N to /CAN1_P. If the switch were intended to join the buses, it should connect P-to-P and N-to-N, not cross the differential pairs.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"5\") (name \"/CAN0_N\") ... (node (ref \"R34\") (pin \"1\") ...)) (net (code \"145\") (name \"Net-(R34-Pad2)\") (node (ref \"R34\") (pin \"2\") ...) (node (ref \"SW6\") (pin \"1\") ...)) (net (code \"6\") (name \"/CAN0_P\") ... (node (ref \"SW6\") (pin \"4\") ...)) (net (code \"8\") (name \"/CAN1_P\") ... (node (ref \"SW6\") (pin \"3\") ...))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"7\") (name \"/CAN1_N\") ... (node (ref \"R35\") (pin \"1\") ...)) (net (code \"146\") (name \"Net-(R35-Pad2)\") (node (ref \"R35\") (pin \"2\") ...) (node (ref \"SW6\") (pin \"2\") ...))"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If SW6 is closed, the two CAN buses are cross-coupled through 120 Ω termination resistors, causing differential loading/shorting and likely CAN communication failure. If left open, the intended switchable termination is not applied as designed.",
      "confidence": 0.62
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design files contain a connectivity deviation: the isolated CAN transceivers have both isolated ground pins tied to common GND, defeating the specified isolated CAN architecture. A second likely deviation is that the switchable CAN termination network appears cross-wired through SW6."
}

